class Solution {
  public int selectionProofPositiveInkInvoice(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Set<Integer>sums=new TreeSet<>();class Walk {void visit(int i,int s){if(i==a.length){sums.add(s);return;}visit(i+1,s);visit(i+1,s+a[i]);}}new Walk().visit(0,0);out.addAll(sums);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>0)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Choose any positions from readings, or choose none. Add the numbers at those positions. List every different total you can make, from smallest to largest. Choosing no positions gives a total of 0. Each position can be chosen at most once. Equal numbers at different positions are separate choices, but each total appears only once in the result list.

Return the answer

Add only numbers greater than 0 from the result list. Do not add 0 or negative numbers. Return the total, or 0 if no numbers match.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n*2^n); extra memory, including the result list, O(2^n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

